From: price@price.demon.co.uk (Michael Clive Price)
Date: Sat, 26 Nov 1994 01:53:10 GMT
Subject: [#94-11-411] ENVIRONMENT: Is the Sun "exploding"?  Was: Nature's real plan

Amara Graps writes:
> Have you been following the recent reports of the Hubble Observations
> that have resulted in newer calculations of the Hubble constant?

I was looking them up today, in Nature, in the library, funnily enough. 

> These newer Hubble constant calculations show the age of the Universe
> to be about 8 billion years old.

They show that the universe is expanding faster than we previously
thought.  The to-young age of universe is extrapolated by assuming that
the expansion is slowing down at a rate given by Einstein's equations
with an arbitrary factor, called the cosmological constant, set to zero.

> Astronomers used to place the oldest
> stars (Population II) as 10-20 billion years old. How can the age of
> the Universe be younger than that of the oldest stars? The astronomers
> are in a tizzy about this, as you can imagine.

The age of the oldest stars seems authenticated almost beyond doubt. 
Famous last words, I know, yet it does seem established that there are
stars and globular clusters of at least 12 billiion years.  If we accept
the lastest Hubble results then we must assume that the expansion is not
slowing down as fast as most astronomers have thought.  Simply set the
cosmological constant to the required value and you can get almost any
age for the universe over about 8 billion years.  Most folks, for some
reason, like to set the cosmological constant to zero, but there is
really no reason to do so.  Einstein only set the cosmological constant
to fit the empirical data from the real Hubble.  Before then he had set
it to be compatible with a static, eternal universe, so we needn't worry
about adjusting it again.

