From: EdRegis@aol.com
Date: Sun, 27 Nov 1994 12:35:23 -0500
Subject: [#94-11-413] NANOTECH: Drexler on self-replication

Nick Szabo (Fri, Nov 25, 1994 10:46 PM EST) writes:

>Exponential growth is limited by the degree of closure.

So far as I'm aware, exponential growth is not a necessary element of
Drexler's nanotechnology.  When Drexler talks about it in Engines (p. 58,
paperback edition), the prospect of regularly-doubling replicators is
portrayed as a threat, not as something that's desirable.  In Nanosystems,
exponential growth is neither talked about nor covertly assumed as a
necessary component of nanotechnology, so you're really flogging dead horses
here.  What's important about NT is molecular assemblers manufacturing things
to specs, not self-replication, and certainly not "exponential growth."    

In any case, your statement quoted above seems false.  Exponential growth can
occur so long as the products are regularly greater in quantity (in some
units) than the producers; there is no requirement that the members of the
population be of the same type.  For example, if one robot of type X produces
two robots of type Y, and each of them produces two robots of type Z, then
you still have exponential growth.  "Closure," therefore, is not needed.  

