At 03:02 PM 11/25/94, szabo@netcom.com wrote:
[...]
 The rate of 
>change of information distance in technology (designs, tooling interfaces, 
>etc.) is roughly proportional to the number and degree of educated
>humans.  In the history of technology, large amounts of useful information 
>have never been created in a short period of time.  Information must be
>heavily digested, cross-referenced, and recombined in numerous failed
>experiments before useful forms, which are substantially similar
>to previous forms, appear.  Slow change in information will continue
>to be the main objective phenomenon underlying human civilization.
[...]
"Singularity" is mere 
>millenarian superstition.
>
>Over the entire history of civilization, information distance has
>never changed dramatically in a short period of time.  (I challenge 
>readers to provide counterexamples).[...]

>I can predict with reasonable confidence that the major religions in 
>the world will still be Christianity, Islam, Hinduism and Bhuddism, and
>that C (including the C++ variant) will still be a major computer programming 
>langauge in 2010.  [...]

That one is almost certainly false, if you mean that C/C++ will keep its
current "market share". I would say that languages like that will be
in 2010 what assemblers are today. If you check the wantads, you see
that stuff like Powerbuilder and other 4GLs are finally starting to
get the attention that VHLL optimists predicted they would have had by now.

Anyway, specific predictions are not the point, as Nick said. Objectively,
the amount of published science seems to be growing exponentially, limited,
again per Nick, by the number of working scientists and technologists. While
it is true that scientific breakthroughs take a while to digest into inventions,
I also see that not all breakthroughs and all inventions have equal impact,
even approximately. If you look at the history of transportation for one
example, say you graph speed of commonly-available conveyances over time, I'd
say you're going to get yet another S-curve (exponential growth damped by
one or more limits), but a nice steep one.

Available energy is another one, one that Simon seized on. From ~4 Myr. BP
to ~1.5MyBP, people had available only digestive energy and solar energy
incident on the body. The niches that they could occupy were circumscribed.
The taming of fire brought about the first great expansion, into temperate
climates. A small jump came from the invention of the oil lamp, but was a
long time before the discovery of powerful chemical energy sources
like gunpowder and naptha made a new generation of weapons and vehicles 
possible. Shortly thereafter, we tapped into atomic fission, which was
the largest leap yet.

Neither knowledge nor invention is smooth or slow, even though we may be
frustrated by the lack of progress in a pitifully short human lifetime.

Looked at another way, though, when my father (1907 - 1993) was a baby,
the fastest transport he had was a horse and wagon (some bike mechanics
had flown a flimsy contraption a few years before and 500 miles East,
and rich folks could afford to ride wood-powered trains at ~45 MPH).
His lifespan included supersonic atmospheric flight, nuclear arms and
power plants, and a manned moon landing.

Another angle from which Nick's vision might be improved on is information
technology itself. Since we are busy building a planetary Web with all
sorts of data almost instantly available, we might want to reflect that
retooling and redesign of the ways we disseminate information itself has
more leverage than other technological processes. The dampers on many of
our growth processes include the economic and political (e.g., why we don't
have atomic-ion rockets), and are therefore
fuzzy to predict or control, but a big damper has always been the collection
and correlation of information across disciplinary boundaries (this is 
why if you want an atomic bomb you collect the best people in all the
relevant disciplines and lock them in a secret facility until they
mushroom for you).

