From: hanson@hss.caltech.edu (Robin Hanson)
Date: Sun, 27 Nov 1994 12:55:55 -0800
Subject: [#94-11-419] NANOTECH: Drexler predicts when it will happen

EdRegis@aol.com writes:
>Okay.  Still, the nature and number of predictions one needs to make in order
>to live happily and well are pretty few: e.g., the road will continue on the
>other side of the hill, etc.  But as for projecting what's going to be
>happening in 2010, 2040, or whatever, it ought to be recognized that all such
>forecasts are mere guesswork.  And no matter how they turn out, they have
>little or no effect on our lives today, so what's the point, really?  

I'm not sure what you mean by "mere guesswork" (is this better than
random prediction?).  Sure, there are very short time scales over
which we can make very good predicitons, and there is likely some long
time scale where our predictive ability is so bad, and the
consequences for our lives now so few, that there isn't much point in
thinking about that time.

But I don't see any way you can really know just what these two
timescales are without *trying* to project on different time-scales,
and seeing how far you can get.  Nor can you know what future times
matter until you try to think about them.  Maybe you tried to think
about some timescale, and couldn't get very far, and reasonably gave
up.  But that doesn't mean someone else couldn't try a different
approach and do better.  

You can't pick a magic date, be it 2010, 2040, or whatever, and then
ignore anything anyone says about after that date, confident that
nothing interesting can ever be said.  There is no short-cut to
evaluating each new proposed analysis on its own terms - if it is
wrong, there should be some flaw internal to the analysis.

In physics, one might say that so few proposed new theories turn out
to be right that there is no point in considering new theories.  But
that would be silly - the value of the few good ones are more than
enough.  Similarly, while there really are only a few powerful
insights about the future, to me they make it all worthwhile.

Robin Hanson

